p.src:
The left side of the Euler–Lagrange equation is $\frac{d}{dt}
\left(
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot x^m}
\right)
=
m\frac{du_m}{dt}
-e\frac{dA_m(x(t),t)}{dt}.$ The potential changes for two reasons: it may depend explicitly on time, and the particle moves through its spatial variation. The chain rule gives $\frac{dA_m}{dt}
=
\partial_0A_m
+\dot x^n\partial_nA_m.$ In longhand, the second term means $\dot x\,\frac{\partial A_m}{\partial x}
+\dot y\,\frac{\partial A_m}{\partial y}
+\dot z\,\frac{\partial A_m}{\partial z}.$

